{"name":"Carnation Festival","hex":"#915870","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carnation","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of Eurasian plant widely cultivated for its flowers.\noriginally, Dianthus caryophyllus\nother members of genus Dianthus and hybrids","originally, Dianthus caryophyllus"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a rosy pink or red colour.","Of a human flesh color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carnation"},{"term":"festival","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Pertaining to a feast or feast day; festive."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A feast or feast day, now especially a special event or series of events celebrating such occasions.","An event or series of special events centred on the celebration or promotion of some theme or aspect of the community, often held at regular intervals."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/festival"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dianthus caryophyllus","blocks":[{"text":"Dianthus caryophyllus, commonly known as carnation or clove pink, is a species of Dianthus native to the Mediterranean region. While its exact natural range is uncertain due to extensive cultivation over the last 2,000 years, wild carnations are most common in the Mediterranean region. Carnations are prized for their wide variety of colors, delicate fringed petals, and fragrance, often described as spicy, clove-like, or reminiscent of a combination of cinnamon and nutmeg, hence the common name \"clove pink\". This aroma has made carnations a popular choice for use in perfumes, potpourri, and scented products. Culturally, carnations are associated with affection, distinction, and maternal love, with variations depending on color and area within its wide geographical range."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/W_carnation4051.jpg/330px-W_carnation4051.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dianthus_caryophyllus","wikidata":"Q158984"},{"title":"Festival","blocks":[{"text":"A festival is an event celebrated by a community and centred on one or more characteristic aspects of its religion, traditions or cultures. It is often observed as an indigenous or national holiday, a religious celebration, or a cultural fair (mela). A festival is a typical case of glocalization, and also reflects the high culture-low culture interrelationship. In addition to religion and folklore, a significant source for festivals has been agriculture. Since, food is such an essential resource, many festivals are associated with harvest time. Religious observances and thanksgiving for good harvests are integrated into celebrations that take place in autumn, such as Halloween in the northern hemisphere and Easter in the southern hemisphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Holi_Festival_of_Colors_Utah%2C_United_States_2013.jpg/330px-Holi_Festival_of_Colors_Utah%2C_United_States_2013.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Festival","wikidata":"Q132241"},{"title":"Shades of pink","blocks":[{"text":"Pink colors are usually light or desaturated shades of reds, roses, and magentas which are created on computer and television screens using the RGB color model and in printing with the CMYK color model. As such, it is an arbitrary classification of color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Color_icon_pink_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_pink_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_pink","wikidata":"Q19847189"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Stilleben._Frukt._Binoit_-_Skoklosters_slott_-_22175.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org